 "notes": [
  {
   "id": "adpendix-consequia",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Imagery",
   "quote": "sublimis evectionis adpendix miseranda ... pendulis comitatus extrema consequia",
   "trans": "a pitiable appendage of the lofty flight ... the furthest retinue of the hanging escort",
   "body": "Psyche, clinging to Cupid’s leg as he soars, is reduced to two startling metaphors: a <em>miseranda adpendix</em> (\"a pitiable appendage\") and the <em>extrema consequia</em> (\"the very tail of the retinue\") of his flight. She is no longer a person but a dangling afterthought of the god.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The metaphors <em>adpendix</em> and <em>consequia</em> dehumanise Psyche into a mere appendage and trailing escort of Cupid’s flight, the pathos of <em>miseranda</em> stressing her helpless, undignified plight as she is dragged through the air.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "immemor-iusserat",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Dramatic Revelation",
   "quote": "parentis meae Veneris praeceptorum immemor ... iusserat",
   "trans": "heedless of my mother Venus’s commands ... she had ordered",
   "body": "Cupid reveals the back-story: Venus had <em>ordered</em> (<em>iusserat</em>) him to make Psyche fall for a worthless man, and he disobeyed (<em>immemor</em>) to take her himself. The long relative clause unpacks the plot the reader has been missing.",
